    "result_text": "Green on a clean build. Here's how I addressed the review:\n\n## Agreed and fixed: dead code removed\nThe review's second point was correct. Since dropping the household-rejection step is intended, I deleted the four now-orphaned artifacts rather than leaving them stranded:\n- `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique.java`\n- `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateException.java` + `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateExceptionHandler.java`\n- the `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateException.yml` escalation handler\n\nI also updated the five javadoc comments that `{@link}`-referenced the deleted class (`AssignHousehold`, `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique`, `DeleteOwner`, `CheckPossibleDuplicate`, `OwnerHousehold`) so nothing dangles and the descriptions match current behaviour. (A stale `target/classes` copy of the deleted YAML briefly broke startup \u2014 resolved with a clean build.)\n\nNote: `sharesHousehold` is *not* dead \u2014 it still has a live purpose in `CheckPossibleDuplicate`, where a declared member is exempt from the possible-duplicate flag. So I kept it.\n\n## Respectfully disagree: can't restore `checkHousehold`\nThe review's primary recommendation \u2014 \"restore the `checkHousehold` step, keep the diff purely additive\" \u2014 would reintroduce a test failure, so I left it removed. The acceptance test `coreCapsLevelByHousehold` creates two **undeclared** members of the same household (`sharesHousehold` unset) and asserts both creates succeed; `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` returns 409 for exactly that case (I confirmed this \u2014 it was the original failure). The step's \"one member per household\" rule is logically incompatible with the spec's own premise of a \"current maximum membershipLevel **among their household members**\" (plural). So the removal isn't an unrelated tidy-up that can be split off \u2014 it's what the spec/test require. Distinct-person protection is unaffected: `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` and `CheckPossibleDuplicate` still catch true identity duplicates.\n\n## Non-blocking note: acknowledged, left as-is\nThe triplicated `findAll()`+filter-by-`householdId` loop predates this change and each copy has a distinct purpose; consistent with existing style, so I left it. If a shared `OwnerHousehold` helper is ever introduced, `CapMembershipLevel` should adopt it too.\n\nThe additive feature (persisted `membership_level_cap` column, cap applied in `MembershipLevel.of`, new `CapMembershipLevel` step) is unchanged. Full suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
